<BaseLayout title="Buying a home on the islands" description="How buying a home on the Kestrel Sound islands works: viewings around the ferry, the inspections that matter, and second homes." plainTop>
  <Prose pretitle="Buying" title="Buying a home on the islands" lead="Island houses are bought on ferry time. Here is how a purchase goes, and what to check that a mainland buyer might miss." image="tile-living.webp" alt="A living room with a stone fireplace and glass doors open to a deck over the bay">
    <h2>How a purchase goes</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>A first call.</strong> Which island, how you will live there, and what you want to spend. Twenty minutes.</li>
      <li><strong>A viewing day.</strong> Four to six houses planned around the ferry schedule, with lunch in Teal Harbor.</li>
      <li><strong>The offer.</strong> Island sales usually include well, septic and bluff inspections as conditions. Dana writes them in.</li>
      <li><strong>Inspections and closing.</strong> Thirty to forty-five days, with the inspectors booked the day the offer is accepted.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>What to inspect</h2>
    <p>Wells, septic systems, bluffs and anything metal near salt water. Ask for the well flow test and a water quality report, a septic inspection with a pump-out, and a geotechnical report for any home near a bank.</p>
    <h2 id="second-homes">Second homes</h2>
    <p>About a third of island homes are second homes. If you plan to rent yours out in summer, check the island's short-term rental rules before you make an offer, and budget for a caretaker who checks the house after winter storms.</p>
    <button type="button" class="btn btn-solid" data-open-contact>Plan a viewing day</button>
  </Prose>
</BaseLayout>
